{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/binary-color-role-mapping.md", "content": "# Binary-color role mapping from sparse demos

A two-color task can encode a global color-role swap rather than a spatial pattern. If a demo has a dominant background color and a sparse contrasting marker color, and its output shows background cells recolored to the marker color while the marker cells become 0, test the same cellwise mapping on the current test. Preserve grid dimensions and map both colors consistently; do not retain sparse marker locations unchanged merely because they resemble foreground objects.

A nearly uniform demo can still be informative: although it gives little evidence about spatial structure, the changed background and marker values reveal the palette mapping. Use a second example when available to verify the same mapping in a less degenerate pattern. Treat frequency as a clue to color roles, not as a rule unless demos establish that the most frequent color is the background. Verify exact output dimensions, row lengths, and values before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544721/attempts/cad7481303e4469588333dc46b5ca8fd/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/binary-color-role-mapping.md"}
